            <td data-label="Typical pay range">{row.range}</td>
            <td data-label="What drives the premium">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A Total Rewards Head or a VP of Talent Acquisition at a large company regularly out-earns a CHRO
      at a mid-size or smaller company. If your only goal is maximum pay, going deep into compensation,
      analytics, or senior talent-acquisition leadership can beat chasing the CHRO title specifically.
      If your goal is enterprise-wide influence and board-level exposure, the generalist-to-CHRO ladder
      still matters more than pure pay comparison. Either way, <strong>this is what unlocking high
      income opportunities in HR actually looks like</strong>: fewer people chase the specialist depth,
      so fewer people compete for the pay it commands.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="entry-qualifications">Entry routes: BBA, MBA-HR, and no-degree paths</h2>
  <p>
    The honest starting point matters more than the prestige ladder people assume you need. Here is
    what each real qualification actually unlocks.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
