  <h2 id="roadmap">A realistic roadmap to your first HR role</h2>
  <p>
    Here is what an honest plan looks like, whether you are still in college or considering a switch
    into HR. The exact pace depends on your starting point — some people move through the early stages
    in a focused stretch of months, others need a couple of years, and both are normal.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pick a starting lens early:</strong> decide whether recruitment, generalist HR, L&D, or HR operations feels closest to work you would actually enjoy, since most people enter through one of these before branching further.</li>
    <li><strong>Build one real, checkable proof point now:</strong> a job description you wrote, a mock recruitment funnel you ran for a friend's small business, or a basic attrition-analysis spreadsheet using public data.</li>
    <li><strong>Get an internship inside your target lens, even unpaid or stipend-only:</strong> real exposure to a live hiring cycle or a real onboarding process teaches judgment faster than most courses do.</li>
    <li><strong>Add a matching certification once your foundation is solid:</strong> SHRM-CP for broad credibility, or a branch-specific course once you have picked a specialisation direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Get outside validation before every real application:</strong> show your work to someone already working in HR and use their feedback to close gaps.</li>
    <li><strong>Build basic AI-tool fluency as a stated skill, not a hidden one:</strong> be ready to talk in an interview about how HR tech and AI tools are already changing recruitment, analytics, or L&D work.</li>
  </ul>

